WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.560
How does a cell recognize an enemy it has never met, and answer within minutes?

2
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.440
A macrophage is a white blood cell that swallows microbes.

3
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.380
Within minutes of touching a bacterium, it begins to change its gene expression.

4
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.620
So what links a touch at the membrane to genes in the nucleus?

5
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.040
The answer was waiting in the cytoplasm the whole time.

6
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:35.233
The answer is NF-κB, a transcription factor.

7
00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:40.053
It is a pair of proteins that bind DNA and switch genes on.

8
00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:43.913
In a resting cell, it is already made and folded.

9
00:00:44.533 --> 00:00:51.153
It waits in the cytoplasm, held by its inhibitor, IκB. IκB covers the

10
00:00:51.153 --> 00:00:54.193
signal that would send NF-κB into the nucleus.

11
00:00:55.117 --> 00:01:01.677
A bacterial molecule, lipopolysaccharide, holds two Toll-like receptors side by

12
00:01:01.677 --> 00:01:10.177
side. The pair docks an adaptor, MyD88, which stacks with IRAK kinases into a tower.

13
00:01:11.177 --> 00:01:17.997
An activated IRAK binds TRAF6, which builds a ubiquitin chain joined through lysine

14
00:01:17.997 --> 00:01:24.937
63. The chain is a platform that gathers a kinase, TAK1, next to IKK.

15
00:01:26.377 --> 00:01:30.237
TAK1 switches on IKK, the IκB kinase.

16
00:01:31.117 --> 00:01:35.337
IKK adds two phosphates to IκB.

17
00:01:35.977 --> 00:01:39.977
Even then, IκB still holds NF-κB.

18
00:01:40.737 --> 00:01:44.397
A second ligase adds a chain joined through lysine 48.

19
00:01:45.237 --> 00:01:49.437
That chain sends IκB to the proteasome, which destroys it.

20
00:01:50.177 --> 00:01:54.677
Now nothing covers the signal that sends NF-κB into the nucleus.

21
00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:00.470
Now NF-κB, already folded, enters the nucleus.

22
00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:06.770
The two proteins wrap around the DNA, and each reads one half of its site.

23
00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.210
NF-κB switches on its genes.

24
00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:14.390
Nothing had to be transcribed or translated first.

25
00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:18.270
So NF-κB reaches its genes within minutes.

26
00:02:19.283 --> 00:02:25.383
Take a cell line with no NF-κB activity, and block its protein synthesis.

27
00:02:27.143 --> 00:02:29.743
Lipopolysaccharide still makes the activity appear.

28
00:02:30.203 --> 00:02:33.743
So the protein must already have been there, inhibited.

29
00:02:34.963 --> 00:02:41.183
Here is the rule. Finish the activator, hold it outside the nucleus, and remove its

30
00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:42.843
inhibitor when the signal comes.
